fm — cheatsheet

One pane, one directory. The cursor is remembered per directory by the
name under it, so coming back lands on that file, and a rebuild — a
hidden toggle, a file made or gone — keeps it. Everything else is one
of five sources — the selection, the file under the cursor, the clip,
the directory fm stands in, or cur, the row as the shell beside you
reads it — and every key takes exactly one of them.

move — h j k l are the arrows
  h backspace  up a directory; as thumbs, h is a card back
  l enter      into it, or open the file; as thumbs, l is a card on
  j k          step; as thumbs, a row of cards
  click        the row, the card, or a folder of the path on top, the
               way up; a right click or a double tap is enter on it, and
               a middle click hands its path to the shell's prompt. a
               key on top, t layout, is a button, and so is the layout
               not in use
  g G          first file, last file
  pgup pgdn    page
  z            zoxide query
  '            mark here
  tab          swap the mark and here; unmarked, the last directory
  `            home

find
  /      search names, substring, case-insensitive
  n N    next, prev — wraps
  f      by name, fuzzy, over the tree
  s      by content, fuzzy
  m      by memory — what was said out loud, newest first, done again
  .      show hidden, . and .. among them

f and s select and act on nothing: they put the cursor on what you
picked, and the verb is the next key — enter, e, o, y. A content hit
keeps its line, so e opens there.

m acts. A log line is a verb and then an address — the command that made
it — so picking one says it again: m runs the verb on the address, from
where fm already stands. The address is whole, so the verb carries its
own place and the pane does not follow it; the pick is the act, not the
trip. Only a c line moves the cursor — c was a select, and a select done
again is the landing — so pick one of those when going there was the
point; fm -f, which follows cur, goes wherever the verb said. Only c
writes that log, so m finds what was said out loud, and b is how a row
you merely browsed gets in.

A name that starts with a number, 01-a, is in an order: numbered rows
stand first and in it, a folder among files, for the number is the order
of a todo's nodes and of a tangle tree's chunks. Then folders, then files.

. and .. are entries in the directory like the rest — ls skipped the
two by their leading dot, and the hidden file is that accident. Under
the cursor they make a directory a target like any other: y its path,
c or x it whole, * or = it. On .. enter is backspace; on . it stays. d refuses
both, as rm(1) does. A first visit lands past them.

act
  space    select, then step down
  c x      copy, cut — both fill the clip and clear the selection
  p        paste the clip here, asking a name on a clash: nothing
           typed keeps it pending, and only the name typed again
           writes over the one there
  L        symlink the clip here
  u        drop the clip
  d        delete, after a y
  r        rename
  i I      new file, new directory
  b        bookmark — say the row out loud, into the log m walks
  *        toggle +x
  y Y      yank the path, yank the contents
  = +      clone under ~/Unsynced and go there; + for a dir's contents
  shift-arrow
           earlier, later, up or left, down or right: a numbered row
           swaps its number with the one it passes, 02-b becoming 01-b,
           two renames

  selection    c x d
  cursor       enter e o b r y * = + F shift-arrow
  the clip     p L
  here         ; a v V i I

The header carries all three — [n] selected, cp:n or cut:n pending,
mark:name. A cd clears the selection. Nothing clears the clip but u, a
link, or a cut that finished moving.

the pronoun
  fm with no argument opens on cur: the directory it names, landed on
  its own name. From then on the row under the cursor is cur, so o, e
  and p at the prompt beside you mean this file without being told
  which — and `{c} hands it to anything else. A file made, renamed or
  pasted is landed on, so cur follows what a verb made.

  Only the pane you are looking at says it. A background fm — scrolled,
  or woken by a resize — would take the shell's this, so it keeps quiet
  until the pane has the eye and says its row when it gets it back,
  unless cur names that row already, perhaps with a line. The log m
  walks is c's alone: what was said out loud, not every j.

  fm -f follows cur as well, as ed -f does, and a frame runs it so: when
  another pane says cur, a click, a verb at the prompt, fm stands in the
  folder that holds it, on it — a folder too, for cur is a row — and a
  file's line rides along for e.

the shell — both directions
  o     from a prompt: open cur, the row fm is on, in a pane beside
        you; the directory you are in while nothing is said
  ;     from fm: a shell in a pane beside you, in the directory fm
        is in

q closes the pane and the shell keeps the line you were typing. fm does
not move the shell or type at it.

o is the same word on both sides: the command with no argument takes
cur, and the key hands it the row — a directory row is another fm in a
pane beside you, a file row its app, a text ed. In a frame the frame's
own take them: a text opens in its ed, and a directory is this fm's,
already on it. o never runs a row; a script is text, and opens in ed.

tools — every one a script in sh/, replaceable
  e      edit (e)
  o      open the row beside you, or in the frame's ed (o)
  w      wipe, at home (w)
  F      format (lint -f)
  f s m  the finders (f, s, m — each piped through fzy, which needs
         the terminal: in a window they say so)
  b      say the row out loud (c)
  v      version control (v)
  V      gitk
  a      a copilot session here (a)

a, o and ; open a pane and fm keeps running; in a frame, a and ; open
a terminal in its panel (`studio/cheatsheet`). b runs and only flashes. The
rest borrow the screen until they exit; F and w hold it after, so what
they printed survives the redraw.

file
  q    quit; in a frame, the frame, asking first while ed holds work
       unsaved
  ?    help, generated from the key table

What a key runs is a name, never a path — bin/ is on $PATH, so any of
them is replaced by writing your own and rebuilding nothing.

change it: keys and help are one table,
fm/4-main/11-bind.go — edit, ./build fm.
